Either language is accepted. The program declares the three variables of question 2, asks for the two inputs with a message (output instruction) before each read (input instruction), calculates the total (process instruction) and prints it. Save it as **Calculator.c** or **Calculator.pas**.

**ANSI C**

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    float U;    /* unit price of the item  */
    int   Q;    /* quantity of item bought */
    float T;    /* total                   */

    printf("Give the unit price of the item: ");
    scanf("%f", &U);
    printf("Give the quantity of item bought: ");
    scanf("%d", &Q);

    T = U * Q;
    printf("Total price = %.2f\n", T);
    return 0;
}
```

**Standard (ISO) Pascal**

```pascal
program Calculator(input, output);
var
    U, T : real;       { unit price and total }
    Q    : integer;    { quantity of item bought }
begin
    write('Give the unit price of the item: ');
    readln(U);
    write('Give the quantity of item bought: ');
    readln(Q);

    T := U * Q;
    writeln('Total price = ', T:1:2)
end.
```

Points to notice: the variables are declared before use (`float U; int Q; float T;` in C, the `var` section in Pascal); in C, `scanf` needs the address operator `&` and the format `%f` for a `float`, `%d` for an `int`; the process instruction is the assignment `T = U * Q;` in C and `T := U * Q;` in Pascal (the Pascal assignment sign is `:=`, and there is no semicolon before `end.`); `%.2f` and `T:1:2` print the total with two decimal places. The same program with `int U, Q, T;` and `%d` also solves the problem for whole numbers. (The programs were not compiled; the logic was checked with a Python translation, see the note on the verification.)

*Marking (10 marks, proposed breakdown):* declaration of the variables with suitable types (3 marks); input instructions with messages (3 marks); process instruction T = U x Q (2 marks); output instruction (1 mark); correct syntax and program structure (1 mark).
